course: dictionary
lesson: 13931
title: Real, fair, or allowed: the word legitimate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Legitimate means allowed by the rules, fair and proper, or real and not fake. Read the words around it to find which meaning fits. «Mum said I had a legitimate reason to be late, because the bus broke down.» A good, true reason that people accept is a legitimate reason.
explain: Try the clues in a sentence like this one: «Before you type your password, check that the website is legitimate.» Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Real, or fair? Put your meaning in the sentence. If «real, not a scam» makes sense, you have found it.
explain: Legitimate is a long, formal word. Friends often shorten it to «legit», as in «That was legit funny.» That means really funny, but in school writing you should use the full word.
fact: Legitimate means allowed by law or by the rules, and fair and proper.
fact: «A legitimate reason» is a good, true reason that people accept as fair.
fact: «A legitimate website» is a real one, not a scam or a fake copy.
fact: Legit is slang, short for legitimate, and means really or truly, as in «legit funny».
fact: In old use, legitimate meant born to parents who were married to each other, but this is rare today.
exercise: «She had a legitimate reason for being late: the bus broke down.» What does legitimate mean here? | *A good, fair and true reason | A very long reason | A secret reason nobody can know | A reason she made up to fool everyone
exercise: «Check that the message is legitimate before you share your password.» What does legitimate suggest? | Funny and silly | Very old | Written in big red letters | *Real, not a scam
exercise: Tom says, «That was legit funny!» What does legit mean in his sentence? | Fake and pretend | *Really, truly, great | Cheap and cheerful | Allowed by the school rules
test: «The shop was legitimate, so we bought the bike there.» What does legitimate mean? | Closed for the day | Very small | *Real and fair, not fake | Fake and pretend
test: «Is there a legitimate reason you did not finish your homework?» Which word could replace legitimate without changing the meaning? | Loud | *Good | Silly | Secret
exam: «The judge said the decision was legitimate because every rule had been followed.» What does legitimate mean? | *Allowed by the rules, fair and proper | Painted in bright colours | Very tired | Sent by post
